    1702	### D9 — Sum-product gain on the prime set in F_p (Bourgain-Glibichuk-Konyagin)
    1703	
    1704	**Question:** for `A_prime = {p_i mod p : p_i prime, p_i < p} ⊂ F_p`,
    1705	the Bourgain-Glibichuk-Konyagin (2006) sum-product theorem gives
    1706	`max(|A+A|, |A·A|) ≥ |A|^{15/14} / (log|A|)^{2/7}` for any `A` with
    1707	`|A| ≤ p^{7/13}(log p)^{-4/13}`. The bound is for ARBITRARY `A`. The
    1708	question: is the *empirical* sum-product gain
    1709	`g(A) := max(|A+A|, |A·A|) / |A|^{15/14}` strictly LARGER for the prime
    1710	set than for matched-cardinality random subsets of `F_p`? If yes,
    1711	primes carry an additive-multiplicative joint structure invisible to
    1712	the existing 35+ pseudorandomness measures (all of which are local /
    1713	1-dimensional). If matched, primes are BGK-saturating in the strongest
    1714	combinatorial sense.
    1715	
    1716	**Why frontier:** sum-product is THE canonical test of "no joint
    1717	structure under both addition and multiplication." Primes ARE the
    1718	multiplicative atoms but their additive structure is constrained only
    1719	by Hardy-Littlewood singular series. **The prime set in F_p has never
    1720	been directly tested as a sum-product problem**; published BGK
    1721	applications all target generic subsets, multiplicative subgroups, or
    1722	random-set baselines (Garaev 2008 is closest, but tests *random*
    1723	A ⊂ F_p, not A_prime). If `g(prime) ≫ g(random)`, the gain has to
    1724	come from the HL mod-q residue distribution → links additive
    1725	combinatorics to the singular series at the *joint* level (orthogonal
    1726	to E2.13 Gowers, which is purely additive). If `g(prime) ≈ g(random)`,
    1727	this is the FIRST sum-product-class pseudorandomness measure of χ_P,
    1728	and the strongest such test (joint-structure invariant).
